bhyve: hda: the sound ring may be a file (play=keel:/path/to/file)

keelOS's ring is a POSIX shared memory object.  macOS limits those names to
31 characters and fails ftruncate() on one that exists, so a restarted guest
could not take its ring again.  A name with a second '/' is now opened as a
file and mapped; the reader maps the same file.  The device name may be as
long as a path.

Applies to audio.c as keelOS has it (keel/audio.c).

--- a/usr.sbin/bhyve/audio.c
+++ b/usr.sbin/bhyve/audio.c
@@ -59,7 +59,7 @@
 	int fd;
 	uint8_t dir;
 	uint8_t inited;
-	char dev_name[64];
+	char dev_name[1024];	/* (a path in the VM's folder is longer than 64) */
 
 	/* keel: play=keel:/keel-snd-NAME */
 	struct keel_snd *ring;	/* NULL: OSS */
@@ -81,9 +81,16 @@
 	void *p;
 	int fd;
 
-	fd = shm_open(path, O_RDWR | O_CREAT, 0600);
+	/*
+	 * A name with a second '/' is a file to map, not a POSIX shared memory object: macOS
+	 * limits those names to 31 characters and refuses to size an existing one again.
+	 */
+	if (strchr(path + 1, '/') != NULL)
+		fd = open(path, O_RDWR | O_CREAT, 0600);
+	else
+		fd = shm_open(path, O_RDWR | O_CREAT, 0600);
 	if (fd == -1) {
-		warn("hda: shm_open %s", path);
+		warn("hda: open %s", path);
 		return (-1);
 	}
 	if (ftruncate(fd, KEEL_SND_HDR + KEEL_SND_RING) == -1) {
